This README describes apibridge, a companion process that exposes cgminer's
existing RPC API (see API-README) as a modern HTTP/WebSocket JSON API,
intended for a future mobile (iOS/Android) dashboard.

WHY A SEPARATE PROCESS

cgminer's own RPC API is a plain-text/JSON protocol over a raw TCP socket:
one request per connection, no TLS, no WebSocket push (see API-README for
the full protocol). That is not something a mobile app's networking stack
wants to talk to directly (App Transport Security on iOS, background
networking on Android, no push without polling).

Rather than embed an HTTP/TLS/WebSocket library inside the cgminer process
itself, apibridge is built as a second, separate binary. cgminer fork/execs
it at startup and supervises it: if it crashes, cgminer respawns it with
backoff; on shutdown or restart, cgminer terminates it cleanly. apibridge's
only connection to cgminer is the existing RPC API on 127.0.0.1 - a bug or
crash in apibridge can never take mining down with it, and a bug in mining
code is never exposed to whatever talks to apibridge.

From an operator's point of view there is still only one thing to run:
cgminer starts and stops apibridge on its own.

BUILDING

apibridge is fully opt-in and not built by default. Add --enable-apibridge
to configure, alongside whatever driver flags you already use, e.g.:

    ./configure --enable-gekko --enable-apibridge

This builds a second binary, "apibridged", installed alongside cgminer.
Building without --enable-apibridge produces a cgminer binary with no
apibridge code compiled in at all (no new symbols, no new binary).

civetweb (the embedded HTTP/WebSocket library apibridge uses) is vendored
under compat/civetweb-<version>/ - no external dependency to install.

RUNNING

apibridge is also opt-in at runtime. Pass --api-bridge to cgminer:

    ./cgminer --api-listen --api-bridge -o stratum+tcp://pool:port -u user -p pass

If --api-listen was not also given, cgminer enables it automatically (with
a warning) since apibridge depends on it.

Relevant flags:

  --api-bridge                Enable the apibridge companion process,
                               default: disabled
  --api-bridge-port <n>       Port for the HTTP/WebSocket listener,
                               default: 4029
  --api-bridge-bind <addr>    Bind address, default: 0.0.0.0 (LAN-reachable)
  --api-bridge-token-file <path>
                               Where to write the generated auth token,
                               default: <install dir>/apibridge.token
  --api-bridge-control        Enable the Phase 2 control endpoints (see
                               below), default: disabled
  --api-bridge-write-token-file <path>
                               Where to write the generated write-scoped
                               token, default: <install dir>/apibridge-write.token

On every cgminer start, a random bearer token is generated and written
(mode 0600) to the token file. Read it to authenticate:

    curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat apibridge.token)" \
         http://<host>:4029/api/v1/summary

The token is not logged and is passed to the apibridge child process via
an environment variable, not argv, to avoid exposure through `ps`. It
rotates on every cgminer restart - there is no persistent pairing yet (see
ROADMAP below).

PHASE 1: READ-ONLY MONITORING

  GET /api/v1/summary   Same data as the RPC "summary" command
  GET /api/v1/devs      Same data as the RPC "devs" command
  GET /api/v1/pools     Same data as the RPC "pools" command
  GET /api/v1/stats     Same data as the RPC "stats" command - per-device
                        detail, including GekkoScience-specific fields such
                        as "Fan" (GSA1/GSA2 fan RPM, from the device's
                        single-byte tach reading) and "FanCeiling" (true
                        once "Fan" hits 7650rpm, the byte encoding's
                        representable ceiling - the true RPM may be higher
                        and the reading wrapped; there is no way to tell
                        from a single byte, so treat "Fan" as "at or above
                        7650" rather than exact once "FanCeiling" is true).
                        Not included in the /stream envelope since it is
                        much larger per device than summary/devs/pools
  GET /api/v1/health    Bridge + cgminer reachability status, no auth
                        required (used for connection-status UI)
  GET /api/v1/stream    WebSocket upgrade; pushes a combined
                        {"type":"stats","summary":...,"devs":...,
                        "pools":...,"stale":...,"ts":...} envelope
                        immediately on connect and once per poll cycle
                        (default every 2s)

All routes except /health require the bearer token, either as
"Authorization: Bearer <token>" or, for the WebSocket route (some mobile
WebSocket clients cannot set custom headers on the upgrade handshake), as
a "?token=<token>" query parameter.

If cgminer becomes unreachable, the last-known-good data keeps being
served with "stale":true rather than an error, so a dashboard has
something to show instead of a blank screen.

PHASE 2: CONTROL ENDPOINTS

Phase 2 relays a whitelisted subset of cgminer's own ascset RPC command
(see API-README) - it does not reimplement any of ascset's validation.
Every value driver-gekko.c already clamps (frequency ranges, voltage
limits, ...) is still clamped by driver-gekko.c; apibridge only decides
which option *names* it's willing to forward at all.

Enable with --api-bridge-control (see RUNNING above). This also requires
cgminer to be started with --api-allow granting write access to
apibridge's own connection, e.g.:

    ./cgminer --api-listen --api-bridge --api-bridge-control \
        --api-allow W:127.0.0.1 -o stratum+tcp://pool:port -u user -p pass

Without that --api-allow rule, cgminer's own RPC ACL (not apibridge's)
rejects every ascset request from apibridge with "Access denied" - cgminer
defaults every API connection, including apibridge's loopback one, to
read-only. apibridge checks this itself once at startup (cgminer's
"privileged" RPC command, which does nothing but confirm write access) and
reports the result as "control_available" on /api/v1/health, so a
dashboard can grey out controls with a clear reason instead of every
request silently failing.

  POST /api/v1/control        Body: {"asc_id":0,"option":"freq","value":650}
                               "value" is omitted for lockfreq/unlockfreq/
                               zeromaxt. "asc_id" is the same ASC index as
                               in the "devs" response.
  POST /api/v1/control/reset  Body: {"asc_id":0} - device reinit, kept as
                               its own endpoint (not reachable through the
                               generic "option" field) since it's more
                               disruptive than the other options.

Whitelisted options for POST /api/v1/control:

  freq        <0-800/1200>   Set frequency (all chips)
  target      <0-800/1200>   Set the auto-tuning target frequency
  corev       <0-500>        Set core voltage (GSA1/GSA2 - Compac A2 /
                              Terminus A2 - only; harmless no-op elsewhere)
  setfan      <0-100>        Set fan speed % (GSA1/GSA2 V2.1/V3 only)
  lockfreq    (no value)     Freeze auto-tuning
  unlockfreq  (no value)     Resume auto-tuning
  zeromaxt    (no value)     Clear the overtemperature-cooldown latch so a
                              GSA1/GSA2 device resumes at full frequency
                              instead of staying throttled (GSA1/GSA2 only;
                              harmless no-op elsewhere)

These endpoints require a second, write-scoped bearer token - separate
from the read-only token above, and only generated/handed to apibridge
when --api-bridge-control is given. Read it the same way:

    curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat apibridge-write.token)" \
         -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
         -d '{"asc_id":0,"option":"freq","value":650}' \
         http://<host>:4029/api/v1/control

The read-only token does *not* work on these routes, and the write token
does not work on the Phase 1 read routes - they are checked independently.
If --api-bridge-control was not given at all, both control routes answer
501 rather than checking any token, so a client can tell "not enabled"
apart from "wrong token".

Every control request - authorized or not, whitelisted or not, whatever
cgminer's own ascset verdict was - is written to apibridge's own log
(stderr, "AUDIT:" prefix) with the source address, asc_id, option, value,
and outcome. cgminer's own ascset response is relayed back close to
verbatim: HTTP 200 with cgminer's message on success, 403 if cgminer's ACL
denied it (the --api-allow requirement above), 422 for any other rejection
from the driver (e.g. an out-of-range value - the message is
driver-gekko.c's own, not re-derived by apibridge), 502 if cgminer was
unreachable.

AUTOMATED TESTS

CI (see .github/workflows/build.yml) runs three layers, none of which need
real hardware:

  - lint job: cppcheck over apibridge/*.c and cgminer-apibridge.c, ruff
    over apibridge/tools/*.py, shellcheck over hw-smoke-test.sh.
  - unit tests: apibridge/tests/test_auth.c, test_settings.c and
    test_control.c, plain assert-based C binaries (no framework)
    exercising the pure logic in auth.c, settings.c, and control.c (the
    Phase 2 option whitelist, wire-parameter construction, and
    STATUS-to-HTTP mapping). Run locally with `make check` after
    `./configure --enable-apibridge`.
  - component test: apibridge/tools/component-test.py starts apibridged
    against a fake, stdlib-only stand-in for cgminer's RPC API and drives
    /health, /summary, /devs, /pools (with and without the bearer token,
    including the ?token= fallback), the Phase 2 control routes (control
    disabled -> 501; enabled with cgminer's ACL granting write access ->
    200/400 whitelist rejection/401 wrong or read-only token; enabled with
    cgminer's ACL denying write access -> control_available:false and a
    403 passthrough), plus a clean SIGTERM shutdown. Run locally with
    `apibridge/tools/component-test.py` once cgminer and apibridged are
    built.

TESTING AGAINST REAL HARDWARE

The component test above covers the HTTP/auth/statscache stack against a
fake cgminer; it still can't exercise real device detection or the
WebSocket stream, since GitHub-hosted runners have no USB access to actual
miners. To exercise the full stack (device detection, REST endpoints, the
WebSocket stream, and clean shutdown) against a physically attached
GekkoScience device, run:

    apibridge/tools/hw-smoke-test.sh <gekko-detect-flag> [pool] [user] [pass]

e.g. for a Compac A1:

    apibridge/tools/hw-smoke-test.sh --gekko-compaca1-detect \
        stratum+tcp://stratum.braiins.com:3333 MyWorker.test x

This is a manual, opt-in script (not run by CI) that starts cgminer
against the given pool with the given device, waits for apibridge to come
up, checks /health, /summary, /devs, /pools (with and without the bearer
token), reads a few live frames off /stream via a small stdlib-only
WebSocket client (apibridge/tools/ws_probe.py), and verifies cgminer can
shut down apibridged cleanly within its SIGTERM grace period. Set
HW_SMOKE_TEST_CONTROL=1 to also start cgminer with --api-bridge-control
--api-allow W:127.0.0.1 and exercise POST /api/v1/control end to end
against the real device via a "zeromaxt" call - chosen because it's a
harmless no-op on a healthy device, unlike freq/corev, so the script
doesn't perturb whatever the device is actually doing. Off by default.

CLI CLIENT

For manual poking around - not a scripted test, just a convenient way to
query a running apibridge instance from a terminal - use
apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py. Stdlib only, no dependencies.

    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py health
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py summary
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py devs
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py pools
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py stream          # follow /stream until Ctrl+C
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py stream -n 5      # 5 frames then exit
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py control --asc-id 0 --option freq --value 650
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py control --asc-id 0 --option zeromaxt
    apibridge/tools/apibridge-cli.py control --asc-id 0 --reset

Each command prints a condensed human-readable view by default, or the
raw JSON response with --json. Connection options (--host, --port) default
to 127.0.0.1:4029. The bearer token is read from --token, --token-file
(default: ./apibridge.token, the same relative path cgminer writes it to
when run from the repo root), or the APIBRIDGE_TOKEN environment
variable, in that order; /health needs no token. The `control` subcommand
additionally reads the write token from --write-token, --write-token-file
(default: ./apibridge-write.token), or APIBRIDGE_WRITE_TOKEN. Run with
--help for the full option list.

SECURITY NOTE

Phase 1/2 have no TLS and bind to 0.0.0.0 by default (LAN-reachable) since
the whole point is a phone on the same network reaching it. Only run this
on a trusted home/lab LAN. Use --api-bridge-bind 127.0.0.1 if you want to
pair it with your own reverse proxy or VPN instead of exposing it directly.
Only pass --api-bridge-control (and the --api-allow W: rule it requires)
on installs where you actually want remote control - it's off by default
precisely because it's a bigger blast radius than read-only monitoring.

ROADMAP

Phase 3 (planned): mDNS/Bonjour discovery for the mobile app, TLS, and a
real per-device pairing flow to replace the current shared-token-file
model.
